1 | go-backend | Fix the test that has turned main red, and report on the class without fixing all of it. THE FAILURE: apps/api/internal/service/recap_confirmation_test.go, TestRecapGate_ConfirmingUnlocksWhenTheRecapAlsoAsked, fails deterministically on current main. It books a hotel with check_in 2026-08-25 and check_out 2026-08-28 at lines 34, 35, 51, 66, 138 and 139. Those dates are in the past as of today, 2026-08-30, so the flow now re-asks for budget and classifies the intent as trip rather than hotels, and the assertions at lines 58, 76, 77, 79 and 80 fail. No production code is wrong. The test expired. THE FIX: make the dates relative to the current date so the test cannot expire again, for example a check-in a fixed number of days in the future computed at test time, with the check-out a fixed span after it. Keep the span the same as today, three nights, because the expected copy mentions 3 nights and the assertion depends on it. Read the surrounding test carefully before changing anything: this file encodes a recap-gate behaviour that took real work to pin, and the point is to keep testing the same behaviour, not to make the assertion pass. If any assertion depends on the literal date string, derive it from the same computed value rather than hardcoding it again. Do NOT change production code. Do NOT weaken or delete an assertion to get green. If you conclude the only way to pass is to change what is asserted, stop and report instead. VERIFY: run the single test 5 times with count=1 and show it green every time, then run the full apps/api suite with race. Paste real exit codes captured directly, never read after a pipe. SECOND, REPORT ONLY, DO NOT FIX: a scan finds 128 test files under apps/api/internal containing hardcoded 2026-0x dates. Some are harmless because they are fixtures never compared against now, and some are time bombs like this one. Do not attempt to fix them in this PR. Instead, open ONE follow-up issue that states the size of the class, names a handful of the most likely time bombs with file and line, and proposes how to find them mechanically, for example a test helper that fails when a fixture date is in the past, or a scheduled job that runs the suite with a clock set forward. THIRD, also report only: state in the PR body that Go Test last passed on main at 0fc77ab7 and was skipped by the path filter on the two commits after it, so main went red with no red run. That gap is worth its own issue too if you judge it separate from the wiring gaps already filed. Open a PR, do not merge. | fix/expired-date-recap-test

2 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch fix/expired-date-recap-test. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a /tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved, report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS MOST: does the test still assert the same behaviour it asserted before, or was it quietly weakened to get green. Diff the assertions before and after, not just the dates. An assertion removed, loosened, or retargeted is a BLOCK-FIX even if the suite is green, because this test pins a recap-gate behaviour that exists to stop the product re-asking for something the traveller already gave. SECOND: verify the fix actually cannot expire. Reason about what happens when the clock moves a year forward, and if you can, prove it by running the test with a shifted clock or by inspecting that every date flows from one computed value with no literal left behind. A fix that merely moves the expiry date further out is not a fix. THIRD: confirm no production code changed. FOURTH: run the single test yourself at least 3 times and the package suite once with race, and report your own exit codes rather than repeating the authors. FIFTH: confirm the follow-up issue about the 128-file class was actually opened and that it names concrete files rather than being a vague note. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC EXPIRED DATE TEST, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | fix/expired-date-recap-test
